{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>et HaShem you have affirmed this day to be</b> etc. The word <b>this day</b> may be explained according to the statement of the Sages, whose memory is for a blessing (Ketubot 110b): ״Everyone who dwells outside the Land is like one who has no god,״ end of quote. And this is what it means by <b>et HaShem you have affirmed this day</b> — specifically, meaning: since they were in the holy Land, and even though they were still in the land of Sihon and Og, with all this we have already written that it is sanctified since it was conquered by a public conquest at the word of a prophet, as we demonstrated in parashat Matot (Numbers 32:3). Therefore its place is not considered outside the Land. And for this reason they affirmed HaShem to be for them a god, which is what it says: ״to be for you a god.״" "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to the statement of the Sages, whose memory is for a blessing (Zohar vol. 1, 108b), that the other lands were delivered into the hand of the celestial officers, whereas the Land of Israel has no judge apart from HaShem. And this is what it says: <b>et HaShem you have affirmed this day</b> — upon your entry into the Land — <b>to be for you a god</b>, in the manner of the verse (Psalms 17:2): ״מִלְּפָנֶיךָ מִשְׁפָּטִי יֵצֵא״ — and our judgment does not come before an officer or judge.״" "<b>Further,</b> the intention may be to say that they affirm HaShem, even though He brings them into judgment to give each man according to his ways and his evil deeds, which is what it says: <b>et HaShem you have affirmed</b> etc. <b>to be for you a god</b> — and it is known that Elohim is the attribute of justice; nevertheless, they affirm HaShem and He blesses even upon evil with something of the nature of good." "<b>And to walk in His ways</b> — meaning: even though HaShem did not command to walk in them, nevertheless when they recognize in them that they are the ways of HaShem, whatever He does, the children of Israel shall do likewise — for example, visiting the sick, burying the dead, and similar things, which we find HaShem doing — they too shall walk in His ways. <b>His statutes (חֻקָּיו)</b> are commandments that have no rational reason. <b>His commandments (מִצְוֹתָיו)</b> are commandments that reason also concurs with. <b>His ordinances (מִשְׁפָּטָיו)</b> are laws that He handed over to be judged between a man and his fellow. <b>And to heed His voice</b> — this is the study of Torah. And Scripture was precise in saying ״His voice״ according to the statement of the Sages, whose memory is for a blessing (Numbers Rabbah, ch. 14), and this is their language: ״From where do we learn that one who hears from the mouth of a minor… it is as though he hears from the mouth of the Almighty, as it is written (Deuteronomy 15:5): ״אִם שָׁמוֹעַ תִּשְׁמַע בְּקוֹל ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ״.״ And the reason He commanded concerning Torah study last is to alert us that even if a person has grasped and fulfilled everything stated above, nevertheless he should not say: ״Why should I study Torah? If the purpose is to know what Israel must do — behold, I have known and fulfilled all the words of HaShem!״ — rather, even so, Scripture obligated Torah study. And this is what it says after everything He obligated to do: <b>and to heed His voice</b> — for the commandment of Torah study is a commandment in its own right, from which one is never exempt until the day of his death, as it is written (Numbers 19:14): ״זֹאת הַתּוֹרָה אָדָם כִּי יָמוּת בְּאֹהֶל״.״"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :register {:flags {3 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {3 "The Midrash quotation in comment 3 is truncated with וכו׳ (״etc.״); rendered with ellipsis to mark the lacuna exactly as in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:19:19.877157031Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}